        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/30/1140927/a-fundamental-flaw-leaves-llms-vulnerable-to-attack/",
        "date":  "2026-07-30",
        "theme":  "Models",
        "summary":  "Researchers have identified a fundamental vulnerability in large language models that makes them impossible to fully secure against attacks. By exploiting how LLMs identify instruction sources through text style rather than role tags, attackers can trick models into providing dangerous information. The vulnerability persists despite security training efforts, as the flaw is inherent to how these systems process language.",
        "execSummary":  "This research delivers a structurally important finding for enterprise AI deployments: the security boundary that separates system-level instructions from user-level inputs in LLMs is enforced by learned style patterns, not by a cryptographically secure access control mechanism ΓÇö and it can be bypassed by mimicking the right writing style. This means any enterprise AI deployment that relies on system prompt instructions to enforce data access boundaries, persona constraints, or prohibited topic rules is operating on a probabilistic guardrail, not a hard control. For customer support AI leaders, the implication is that sensitive policy rules must be enforced at the application layer with deterministic code, not delegated to the model\u0027s instruction-following behavior alone. No current mitigation fully closes this vulnerability.",
